## Problem 1 — a header with two endiannesses

Edit `ex1/solution.ts`. Implement:

```ts
export interface Header {
  magic: string;
  version: number;
  flags: number;
  payloadLength: number;
  checksum: bigint;
}

export function readHeader(buf: Buffer): Header;
```

The 20-byte layout:

| offset | size | field |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 4 | ASCII magic, must be `IPSK` |
| 4 | 1 | uint8 version |
| 5 | 1 | uint8 flags bitfield |
| 6 | 2 | reserved — ignore |
| 8 | 4 | uint32 **big-endian** payload length |
| 12 | 8 | uint64 **little-endian** checksum, returned as a `bigint` |

```ts
readHeader(Buffer.from("4950534b01070000000000100102030405060708", "hex"));
// { magic: "IPSK", version: 1, flags: 7, payloadLength: 16,
//   checksum: 578437695752307201n }
```

A buffer shorter than 20 bytes throws an `Error` whose message contains `20`.
A wrong magic throws an `Error` whose message contains the four characters
actually found. Do not let Node's `ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE` escape — the caller wants
to hear about your format, not about an offset.

```
uv run dojo test node v3na01_buffers_encodings ex1
```

