## Problem 4 — a sink that batches

Edit `ex4/solution.ts`. Implement:

```ts
export interface BatchSink {
  writable: Writable;
  batches(): Buffer[][];
  finalised(): boolean;
}

export function createBatchSink(): BatchSink;
```

A Writable that records what the stream machinery hands it:

- `_write` records a batch of one chunk;
- `_writev` records a batch of every chunk in the call, in order;
- `_final` sets the finalised flag and calls back;
- `batches()` returns the recorded batches, in order.

```ts
const sink = createBatchSink();
sink.writable.cork();
sink.writable.write(Buffer.from("a"));
sink.writable.write(Buffer.from("b"));
sink.writable.write(Buffer.from("c"));
sink.writable.uncork();
sink.writable.end();
// after 'finish':
sink.batches();     // [[<a>, <b>, <c>]]  — one _writev call
sink.finalised();   // true
```

Uncorked, a single `write` followed by `end` produces `[[<a>]]` through
`_write`. The gate exercises both paths and asserts a sink that implements only
`_writev` fails on the single-write path — so implement both.

Record a batch only after you have decided to accept it, and call the callback
exactly once per invocation.

```
