## Review — the flags have a precedence

Edit `review1/solution.ts`. Implement:

```ts
export type StreamState =
  | "errored" | "destroyed" | "ended" | "ending" | "flowing" | "paused" | "idle";

export function describeStream(stream: Readable | Writable): StreamState;
```

Classify a stream from its introspection properties. The precedence, most
specific first:

1. `errored` is set → `"errored"` (an errored stream is *also* destroyed, so
   this must be checked first, or every failure is reported as a clean stop);
2. otherwise `destroyed` → `"destroyed"`;
3. otherwise finished — `writableFinished` or `readableEnded` → `"ended"`;
4. otherwise `writableEnded` (end() called, not yet flushed) → `"ending"`;
5. otherwise, for a readable, `readableFlowing === true` → `"flowing"`,
   `=== false` → `"paused"`, `=== null` → `"idle"`;
6. a writable that is none of the above → `"idle"`.

```ts
const r = Readable.from([1, 2, 3]);
describeStream(r);                      // "idle"   — nobody has read yet
r.on("data", () => {});
describeStream(r);                      // "flowing"
r.pause();
describeStream(r);                      // "paused"

const w = new Writable({ write(_c, _e, cb) { cb(); } });
w.end();
describeStream(w);                      // "ended"  (or "ending" mid-flush)

const bad = new Writable({ write(_c, _e, cb) { cb(new Error("nope")); } });
bad.write("x");
// after the error settles:
describeStream(bad);                    // "errored", never "destroyed"
```

The gate builds a stream in each state, including a Duplex that has ended its
writable half while its readable half still flows, and requires the documented
answer for each.

```
