## Problem 7 — the same thing, without a Transform

Edit `ex7/solution.ts`. Implement:

```ts
export type Stage<I, O> = (source: AsyncIterable<I>) => AsyncIterable<O>;

export function composeStages(stages: Stage<any, any>[]): Duplex;
```

Build a single Duplex from an array of async generator stages using
`stream.compose`, so that the result drops into `pipeline` like any other
stage.

```ts
const batchesOfTwo: Stage<number, number[]> = async function* (source) {
  let batch: number[] = [];
  for await (const n of source) {
    batch.push(n);
    if (batch.length === 2) { yield batch; batch = []; }
  }
  if (batch.length) yield batch;      // this is _flush, and it is just code
};

const doubled: Stage<number, number> = async function* (source) {
  for await (const n of source) yield n * 2;
};

await pipeline(Readable.from([1, 2, 3]), composeStages([doubled, batchesOfTwo]), sink);
// sink sees [2, 4], [6]
```

An empty `stages` array returns a Duplex that passes objects through unchanged.
A stage that throws destroys the composed Duplex with that error, and the
stages after it are not run.

Compare the batcher above with your ex2 answer. The gate runs both against the
same input and requires identical output — the point of the exercise is that
`_flush` becomes "the code after the loop".

```
