## Hint 1

The signature contains the answer to the design question: `push` returns a
promise. That promise is the backpressure. A producer that awaits it slows to
the consumer's rate; a producer that ignores it is beyond your help, and saying
so in the documentation is more honest than pretending the queue is unbounded
in that case.

## Hint 2

The waiter list is the whole mechanism:

```ts
const waiters: Array<() => void> = [];

push(item) {
  queue.push(item);
  drain();
  if (queue.length < limit) return Promise.resolve();
  return new Promise<void>((resolve) => waiters.push(resolve));
}

// wherever an item leaves the queue:
waiters.shift()?.();
```

Release exactly one waiter per item removed. Releasing them all turns the bound
into a suggestion; releasing none is a deadlock.

## Hint 3

Shape: `queue`, `waiters`, `ended`, `parked` in the closure; a `Readable` in
object mode whose `_read` shifts from the queue, pushes, and releases a waiter;
`push`/`end`/`fail` as described. The decision left to you is what `_read`
should do when the queue is empty and the stream has not ended: it cannot push
nothing and it must not push `null`, so it has to leave a note for the next
`push` to deliver directly. Work out where that note lives.

