## Review — the case pipeline refuses

Edit `review1/solution.ts`. Implement:

```ts
export interface PumpOptions {
  end?: boolean;   // default true
}

export function pump(
  source: Readable,
  destination: Writable,
  options?: PumpOptions,
): Promise<void>;
```

The manual transfer, because `pipeline` always ends its destination and
sometimes you must not — a shared log file, a multiplexed socket, an archive
several sources append to in turn.

```ts
const sink = createSharedSink();
await pump(sourceA, sink, { end: false });
await pump(sourceB, sink, { end: false });
await pump(sourceC, sink);                  // this one ends it
sinkContents();                             // A then B then C
```

`pump` must provide everything `pipeline` would have, minus the ending:

- backpressure honoured — the gate asserts `writableLength` never exceeds the
  mark plus one chunk;
- a source error rejects and destroys the source; the destination is left
  **alone** when `end` is `false`, because other writers still need it;
- a destination error rejects and destroys the source;
- with `end: true` (the default) the promise resolves only after the
  destination has finished;
- with `end: false` the promise resolves when the source is exhausted and every
  byte has been accepted, leaving the destination writable.

