## Hint 1

The prompt tells you the requirement — one operation, not two — and the
filesystem gives you exactly one primitive for it. Look at the table of file
system flags and find the one that means "create, and fail if it already
exists". The kernel performs it under a lock; nothing can interleave.

## Hint 2

The flag and its error, with the branch that matters:

```ts
try {
  const handle = await open(path, "wx");
  try { await handle.writeFile(data); } finally { await handle.close(); }
  return true;
} catch (err) {
  if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "EEXIST") return false;
  throw err;
}
```

`err.code`, not `err.message`. Codes are API and stable across versions and
locales; messages are prose that changes.

## Hint 3

Shape: exactly the structure above, with the nested `try`/`finally` around the
write so the handle closes even if writing fails. The decision left to you is
whether to use `open` plus `writeFile` on the handle, or `fsPromises.writeFile`
with a `{ flag: "wx" }` option — the second is one line and gives you no handle
to leak, and it is worth knowing that the option exists before you decide the
explicit version is better.

references.solution.ts:
// v3na05_fs_handles_atomic / ex4 - reference solution (author only, used by `dojo verify`).
import { open } from "node:fs/promises";

export async function createIfAbsent(
  path: string,
  data: Buffer | string,
): Promise<boolean> {
  try {
    // "wx" is O_CREAT | O_EXCL: the kernel performs the check and the create
    // as one indivisible operation. `if (!exists) write` is two operations
    // with a race between them (TOCTOU) - the reason fs.exists was deprecated.
    const handle = await open(path, "wx");
    try {
      await handle.writeFile(data);
    } finally {
      await handle.close();
    }
    return true;
  } catch (err) {
    // Exactly EEXIST. A bare `catch { return false }` would report EACCES,
    // EISDIR and ENOSPC as "already there", hiding a real failure.
    if ((err as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === "EEXIST") return false;
    throw err;
  }
}

